    "title": "WNBA draft results: 2026 picks include Azzi Fudd, Rori Harmon",
    "text": "A general view of the WNBA logo on the court before a WNBA game between the Atlanta Dream and the Connecticut Sun on September 1, 2025, at Mohegan Sun Arena in Uncasville, Conn. Icon Sportswire/Icon Sportswire via Getty ImagesThere has been some Texas ties in the 2026 WNBA Draft.Like last year, the Dallas Wings have the first pick; in 2025 they selected eventual rookie of the year Paige Bueckers. This time around, they picked another UConn Husky: Azzi Fudd. But the second pick was Olivia Miles out of Texas Christian.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Texas women's basketball player Rori Harmon was selected in the third round and several players from Texas or who went to school here were taken as well. And a prominent college program set a draft record. Below, readers can find notable picks, including those from Texas universities, and those related to the Wings and the Connecticut Sun — which will relocate to Houston and revive the Comets. That team will begin play in 2027.There are a variety of changes for the WNBA this year as it enters its third decade. First, expansion teams the Toronto Tempo and Portland Fire get their first picks ahead of inaugural seasons. Second, after months of negotiations, the WNBA and the Player's Association drastically increased rookie salaries from nearly $79,000 a year to a minimum of $270,000. The first round pick will make a cap of $500,000. Article continues below this adOpening night for the WNBA is May 8. Follow along for live updates below: Live updates of 2026 WNBA Draft Rori Harmon hears her name The Texas Longhorns star guard and Houston native was taken in the third round by Washington. Article continues below this ad Texas native drafted Ashton Jackson, out of Duke, is from China, Texas and was taken by Golden State in the second round. UCLA draft picks make history UCLA sets a record with the most players taken in a single draft from one school with Charlisse Leger-Walker selected 18th by Connecticut. She is also the first New Zealander in the WNBA. Article continues below this ad Another Horned Frog drafted To open the second round, the Seattle Storm selected TCU forward Marta Suarez. TCU's Olivia Miles drafted early Miles is selected by the Minnesota Lynx who had the second pick. She is also the first player from a Texas school taken in this year's draft. Article continues below this ad First Pick of WNBA Draft Azzi Fudd is selected by the Dallas Wings as the first overall pick. She is the seventh UConn women's basketball player drafted first and is the second straight Husky taken first in the draft. How to watch 2026 WNBA Draft Time: 6 p.m. CTArticle continues below this adTV channel: ESPN WNBA draft order First Round Dallas - Azzi Fudd, UConn guard2. Minnesota (from Chicago) - Olivia Miles, TCU guard3. Seattle (from Los Angeles) - Awa Fam, Valencia center4. Washington - Lauren Betts, UCLA center5. Chicago (from Connecticut) - Gabriela Jaquez, UCLA guard6. Toronto - Kiki Rice, UCLA guard7. Portland - Iyana Martin, Perfumerias Avenida guard8. Golden State - Flu'Jae Johnson, LSU guard9. Washington (from Seattle) - Angela Dugalic, UCLA forward10. Indiana - Raven Johnson, South Carolina guard11. Washington (from New York) - Cotie McMahon, Ole Miss forward12. Connecticut (from Phoenix) - Nell Angloma, France guard13. Atlanta - Madina Okot, South Carolina center14. Seattle (from Las Vegas) - Taina Mair, Duke guard15. Connecticut (from Minnesota) - Gianna Kneepkens, UCLA guard Article continues below this ad Second Round 16. Seattle (from Dallas) - Marta Suarez, TCU forward17. Portland (from Chicago) - Frieda Buhner, Germany forward18. Connecticut - Charlisse Leger-Walker, UCLA guard19. Washington - Cassandre Presper, Notre Dame forward20. Los Angeles - Ta'Niya Latson, South Carolina guard21. Chicago (from Portland) - Latasha Lattimore, Ole Miss forward22. Toronto - Teonni Key, Kentucky forward23. Golden State - Ashlon Jackson, Duke guard24. Los Angeles (from Seattle) - Chance Gray 25. Indiana - Justine Pissott, Vanderbilt guard26. Toronto (from New York) - Saffron Shiels, Australia forward27. Phoenix - Ines Pitarch-Granel, France guard28. Atlanta - Indya Nivar, North Carolina guard29. Las Vegas - Janiah Barker, Tennessee Forward30. Washington (from Minnesota) - Darianna Littlepage-Buggs, Baylor forward Third Round 31. Dallas - Zee Spearman, Tennessee forward32. Chicago - Tonie Morgan, Kentucky guard33. Connecticut - Sera Williams, UConn forward34. Washington - Rori Harmon, Texas guard35. Los Angeles - Amelia Hassett, Kentucky forward36. Toronto - Charlise Dunn, Davidson guard37. Portland - Taylor Bigby, TCU guard 38. Golden State - Kokoro Tanaka, Japan guard39. Seattle - Grace VanSlooten, Michigan State forward40. Indiana - Jessica Timmons, Alabama guard41. New York - Manuela Puoch, Australia forward 42. Phoenix - Eszter Ratkai, Hungary guard 43. Atlanta - Keija Ran, China guard 44. Las Vegas - Jordan Obi, Kentucky guard45. Minnesota - Lani White, Utah guardArticle continues below this adGet the hookup and your Horns Up with Longhorns coverage.April 13, 2026|Updated April 13, 2026 8:44 p.m.Originally from Chicago, Zoe brings four years of experience to Austin. After double-majoring in journalism and history with a Spanish minor at the University of Colorado and earning a master's degree from Northwestern, she began working in Petersburg, Va. in May 2021. As the sports/education reporter for The Progress-Index, she won the VPA's Best Sports Column Writing award and was named the Best of Gannett in Tier IV Investigative Reporting. In October 2023, she became the digital news producer for the Times-Picayune. She was recently named an APSE fellow and is a member of NABJ.",
